   <title>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Narrative report</narrative>
   </title>
   <description>
    <narrative xml:lang="EN">Reporting is an important management tool which is used to inform relevant government officials and other stakeholders about the results being achieved by individual projects and the programme as a whole. 
For project partners, it is compulsory to use the prescribed Orange Knowledge annual report format in order to report on project achievements. The annual report also provides valuable data on the financial situation of the project. Based on an analysis of the project performance in relation to the project output(s) and expected outcome(s), a revision of the project work plan may be suggested by the project partners. The consequences of a possible revision on the project work plan and budget must be made clear in the annual report.
Nuffic monitors outputs and outcomes and not activities . Therefore, the main focus of the annual report must be on the achievement of the project&#x2019;s output(s) and planned outcome(s). The information provided in the annual reports of the projects is used by Nuffic to monitor the progress of the project and to inform the Ministry of Foreign Affairs and other stakeholders about the output(s) and outcome(s) being achieved by individual projects and the Orange Knowledge programme as a whole. 
Please note that if project progress is in accordance with the original planning, reporting requirements are minimal. Only in case of deviations from the planning, more elaboration is demanded.
In the grant award letter, the dates for submission of the annual reports are mentioned. In case of a three-year project, between 5-7 reports are obligatory.
The first report (0) is a very short report which focuses on specific conditions and/or recommendations in the grant. There is no need to report on progress in achieving outputs and outcomes, which is done in the first annual report. As annex to the report 0, the consortium agreements between the partners should be submitted.
The final report must reflect on the entire project period.</narrative>
   </description>
   <indicator ascending="1" measure="5">
    <title>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">2. Project progress and achievement</narrative>
    </title>
    <description>
     <narrative xml:lang="EN">In this section (maximum 1000 words), please provide a global overview of the project progress **per output**. To what extent have they been achieved? Comment on the activities that have taken place and the most important milestones.</narrative>
    </description>
    <period>
     <period-start iso-date="2020-01-01"></period-start>
     <period-end iso-date="2020-12-31"></period-end>
     <actual value="**Conflict and Women&#x2019;s right, Gender Equality and Peacebuilding**

All the participants have improved their knowledge on social conflict, conflict analysis and how to identify the key driving factors of the persistence of Gender based Violence (GBV) in Afghanistan understanding gender as a concept as well as looking into Religious Fundamentalisms and the impact on women&#x2019;s lives and rights. This training improved the skills of the participants in the strategy of networking and alliances and Non-Violent Communication. This training enabled the participants to carry out their different activities to challenge gender inequality more effectively. This training also helped the participants to understand the role of women in the ongoing Afghan peace process and how they could influence the ongoing peace talks and post-peace talks. Furthermore, they will use the acquired knowledge and shared experiences while their advocacy and lobbying for eradicating GBV and role of Afghan women in the post -conflict settlement in Afghanistan.

**Strengthening of Afghan Women News Agency**

The goal of the project is to strengthen the capacity of the partner organisation in professional media campaigns in the fields of promotion of peace based on human rights and justice with a focus on women role in society. The training to Afghan Women News Agency improved the capacity of the participants in writing and understanding analytical articles, debates, and advocacy for women and human rights. The participants received also on-the-job- coaching to make sure that gained knowledge in well integrated into the Organisation.

**Capacity Building of media actors in Afghanistan in the fields of human rights, gender respect, peace building and advocacy**

The training improved the skills and know of Peace Stability and Social Development Organisation &amp; Afghan Women News Agency Organisation in the fundamental principles of human rights, gender equality, war &amp; victims, peace building and advocacy technics. The participants enabled to integrate gained knowledge into their work. The participants received a back-to-home-assignment. In our evaluation many participants developed manuals to work the gained knowledge into their work as human rights activists. 

&#x200B;

